with q as (
  select $l7_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter insists on separating two \"prices\" of spaceflight. What are they, and which one did reusability move?", "options": ["The physics price (payload fraction, set by the rocket equation and roughly 4%, locked) and the money price (dollars per kilogram to orbit, set by economics) \u2014 reusability left the physics price alone (slightly worse, even) and cut the money price about twentyfold", "The fuel price and the crew price; reusability cut both", "The launch price and the landing price; reusability merged them", "They are the same number measured in different units"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "The physics price asks what fraction of liftoff mass reaches orbit \u2014 about 4%, fixed by the exponential, the exhaust-velocity ceiling, and the ascent budget. The money price asks what a kilogram to orbit costs in currency, and it is set by how the rocket is built, how often it flies, and how many times it is reused \u2014 none of which appear in any equation. For fifty years the field optimized the locked physics price while the unlocked money price sat waiting; reusability moved the money price by 20x while every physical number stood still."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why is \"propellant is nearly free\" the economic foundation of reusability?", "options": ["Because propellant can be recycled after each flight", "Because the thing you consume (propellant) is a fraction of a percent of the launch cost, while the thing you throw away (the vehicle) is nearly all of it \u2014 so recovering the vehicle and re-paying only the cheap propellant makes another flight almost free", "Because fuel is the heaviest part of the rocket", "Because governments subsidize rocket fuel"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Hundreds of tonnes of liquid oxygen and kerosene cost only a few hundred thousand dollars \u2014 under half a percent of a launch. The rocket is like a 250-million-dollar airliner with a 30-dollar tank of gas: you would never scrap the airliner to save the gas. Because the recoverable hardware is nearly all the cost and the unavoidable propellant is nearly free, spending a big chunk of payload to bring the vehicle home is worth it. If propellant were instead most of the cost, reusability would be nearly worthless."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is the \"reusability tax,\" and why is paying it still a good trade?", "options": ["A government fee on reused rockets; it is avoided by launching abroad", "The rocket equation charges roughly 30-40% of the first stage's payload for the boostback, reentry, and landing burns needed to recover it \u2014 a brutal penalty, but worth it because it saves re-building the first stage (about 70% of hardware cost), amortized across 20+ flights", "The extra mass of a heat shield, which halves the payload", "There is no penalty; reusability is free once the rocket is designed"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Reusability does not beat the rocket equation \u2014 it pays it twice, and the second payment (the boostback burn to reverse downrange velocity, the reentry burn to survive the atmosphere, and the landing burn to touch down at zero velocity) comes straight out of payload, about a third of it. That is a real, physical penalty. It is worth paying only because propellant is nearly free and the recovered first stage is the dominant cost: you concede a third of the payload to slash the cost of the most expensive component by roughly 20x \u2014 provided you fly often enough to amortize."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Both the Space Shuttle and the Falcon 9 are \"reusable,\" yet the Shuttle was the most expensive launcher in history (about 54,500 dollars per kilogram) and the Falcon among the cheapest (about 2,700). What does this show?", "options": ["That reusability always makes rockets cheaper, and the Shuttle's figures are wrong", "That \"reusable\" by itself explains nothing \u2014 reusability pays off only alongside cheap manufacturing and a high flight rate, and the Shuttle lacked both (ruinous to build and refurbish, and too complex to fly more than ~4.5 times a year)", "That the Shuttle used more expensive fuel", "That the Falcon 9 has a better exhaust velocity"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The word \"reusable\" is a category that contains both the best and the worst answers. Reusability amortizes the build cost across flights, but only if the vehicle is cheap to build and refurbish (so the savings are large) and flies often (so there are many flights to spread cost across). SpaceX won all three, reinforcing in a virtuous cycle; the Shuttle had reusability but neither cheap manufacturing nor a high flight rate, so its enormous fixed costs were spread across a tenth of the promised flights. None of the three innovations is a physics change."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "According to the Shuttle autopsy, where was the program's economic fate actually decided?", "options": ["On the test stand, by engine failures", "In the requirements phase, in committee rooms, for political and budgetary reasons \u2014 the Air Force cross-range demand that forced the heavy winged orbiter, and the austerity that forced the expendable tank, marginal booster reuse, and fragile tiles \u2014 before any engineering began", "In orbit, by unexpected physics", "In the wind tunnel, by aerodynamic surprises"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Shuttle's engineering was largely superb \u2014 the RS-25 engines, the thermal protection, 135 flights, Hubble, the ISS. It did not matter, because the vehicle was economically doomed by its requirements: the Air Force cross-range mandate forced wings and a 78-tonne orbiter carried to orbit every flight (payload fraction 1.4% vs Saturn V's 4.7%); post-Apollo austerity forced compromises that traded low development cost for high per-flight cost; and the resulting complexity forced a standing army and a death spiral of slow turnaround and low flight rate. The most consequential decisions were requirements decisions made by institutions for non-technical reasons \u2014 and they were irreversible."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "Launch cost to orbit fell by roughly a factor of twenty in a single decade with no change in the underlying physics. Explain how: what stayed exactly the same, what actually changed, and why \"reusability\" alone is an insufficient explanation.", "rubric": "What stayed the same: every locked constraint. The rocket equation is identical (still exponential in Delta-v over exhaust velocity); the exhaust velocity is identical (kerolox, ~340 s, capped by chemistry); the ascent budget is identical (9.4 km/s, fixed by the planet); and the payload fraction of a reusable rocket is if anything WORSE than the expendable Saturn V's, because of the ~30-40% reusability tax. Not one physical number moved. What changed: three reinforcing ECONOMIC innovations \u2014 reusability (recover and re-fly the first stage, amortizing its cost across 20+ flights instead of discarding it), cheap manufacturing (vertical integration, mass-produced engines, no exotic materials, driving build cost down), and high flight rate (100+ flights a year, which both reuses each booster many times and spreads fixed costs thin). They form a virtuous cycle: cheaper flights raise demand, which raises flight rate, which improves amortization, which lowers cost further. Why reusability alone is insufficient: the Space Shuttle was also reusable and was the most expensive launcher ever (~54,500 dollars/kg), because it lacked cheap manufacturing and a high flight rate \u2014 ruinous to build and refurbish, and too complex to fly often enough to amortize its fixed costs. Reusability is a category holding both the best and worst answers; the revolution was the combination, none of it a physics change. The meta-lesson: when the physics is locked, the leverage moves to economics, which can move by orders of magnitude while the physics does not budge."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "State the unit's through-line principle and show how reusability is its largest application. Then explain the two enduring lessons the finale draws \u2014 about the word \"impossible\"/assumptions, and about where big projects really fail.", "rubric": "The through-line: never carry anything through a Delta-v (or a process) it does not need to experience without extracting its full value. It appears throughout the unit \u2014 staging jettisons empty tanks the instant they are dead; the gravity turn lets gravity do the steering instead of spending thrust on it; staged combustion routes the turbine exhaust into the main chamber instead of dumping it. Reusability is the largest application of all: the entire vehicle \u2014 a piece of aerospace perfection accelerated to the edge of space \u2014 was being carried through its own destruction, discarded after eight minutes as the ultimate dead weight. Recovering it applies the principle to the rocket itself, and it was hiding in plain sight for fifty years. First enduring lesson: expendability FELT as fundamental as the rocket equation, but it was a habit of mind, not a law of nature \u2014 a constraint everyone accepted that turned out to be a choice. The physics doors were genuinely locked; the price was never set by the physics, and the assumption that a rocket must be thrown away was simply wrong for fifty years. When a constraint is called fundamental, ask whether it is a law or a habit. Second enduring lesson: the Shuttle shows that the most consequential decisions in a large technical project are often made in the requirements phase, by institutions, for political or budgetary reasons, before engineers touch the hardware \u2014 and they are frequently irreversible. Brilliant execution cannot rescue a doomed requirement; the failure was in the committee room, not the wind tunnel. (Reusability also embodies a strategic pattern: sometimes you win not by fighting a constraint harder but by willingly paying it in one currency \u2014 payload \u2014 to gain overwhelmingly in another \u2014 cost.)"}]$l7_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
